|passage=Goe to prepare the maryages what neede the torchis light? be holde the towres of troy do shyne with '''brandes''' that blase full bright.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1559|translator=w:Jasper Heywood|title=Troas
|passage=Is yet againe thy brest enflamde, / with '''brande''' of venus might}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|rare}} A conflagration.
# {{lb|en|archaic|or|poetic}} A piece of burning wood or peat, or a glowing cinder.
#: {{co|en|to burn something to '''brands''' and ashes}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1513|author=w:Gavin Douglas|title=The Eneados
|passage=The fearful '''brands''' and bleezes of het fire.}}
#* '''1859-1890''', {{w|John Gorham Palfrey}}, ''History of New England to the Revolutionary War''
#*: Snatching a live '''brand''' from a wigwam, Mason threw it on a matted roof.
#* {{RQ:Irving Tour on the Prairies|chapter=VI|page=47|passage=About three o'clock, we came to a recent camping place of the company of rangers: the '''brands''' of one of their fires were still smoking; so that, according to the opinion of Beatte, they could not have passed on above a day previously.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1559|translator=w:Jasper Heywood|title=Troas
|passage=Or when amid the Grecians shippes, / he threw the '''brandes''' of fyre.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[sword]].
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=10|passage=Paradise, so late their happy seat, / Waved over by that flaming '''brand'''.|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=II|poem=Sir Galahad|stanza=I|page=174|passage=The shattering trumpet shrilleth high, / The hard '''brands''' shiver on the steel, / The splintered spear-shafts crack and fly, / The horse and rider reel: {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1890|author=w:Joseph Jacobs|title=s:English Fairy Tales|chapter=Childe Rowland|page=120
|passage=Then Childe Rowland out with his good '''brand''', that never struck in vain, and off went the cow-herd’s head.}}

